00:04:48.991 --> 00:04:54.091
Two-thirds of new U.S. data center projects are aimed at rural ground, data center knowledge

00:04:54.091 --> 00:04:59.171
reports, while nearly nine-tenths of the halls already built still sit in developed areas.

00:04:59.771 --> 00:05:02.931
Take that split apart at the power lead, not at the zip code.

00:05:02.931 --> 00:05:09.811
old site picks stacked three tests, power already on the grid, users close enough, and a fast network

00:05:09.811 --> 00:05:16.171
in reach. That stack filled northern Virginia, Texas, California, and other urban hubs. A rural

00:05:16.171 --> 00:05:22.631
parcel fails those tests on a map. Users are farther, so latency climbs, and both the substation and the

00:05:22.631 --> 00:05:29.271
fiber may be thin or simply distant. Power is the test that gave way. Public grids, including grids in

00:05:29.271 --> 00:05:33.831
rich, highly developed regions, cannot always deliver what a new hall asks for.

00:05:34.351 --> 00:05:39.351
Operators are looking at generation they own, a dedicated plant or machines on the site.

00:05:39.831 --> 00:05:44.071
Once the electrons are made on the property, a weak local grid is no longer a veto.

00:05:44.671 --> 00:05:45.971
Land moves the same way.

00:05:46.571 --> 00:05:49.151
Big empty urban parcels are scarce and costly.

00:05:49.791 --> 00:05:51.291
Rural ones are easier to find.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:54.711
Permitting meets fewer neighbors, and opposition can be thinner.

00:05:55.351 --> 00:05:56.971
Fiber does not get the same pass.

00:05:56.971 --> 00:06:04.371
The hull still needs a high-performance network, which usually means fiber-optic cable, and a lot of rural ground has little of it.

00:06:04.811 --> 00:06:10.251
Extending that cable is typically cheaper and simpler than a long power line or a major on-site plant.

00:06:10.711 --> 00:06:14.951
If fiber already runs within a few hundred miles, the tie-in can still pay.

00:06:15.551 --> 00:06:21.631
Generation covers the electrons. Those miles of fiber cover the packets. Neither stands in for the other.

00:06:21.631 --> 00:06:25.411
The two-thirds number counts projects, not energized halls.

00:06:26.051 --> 00:06:30.891
Nearly nine-tenths of what already exists is still in developed areas, so nobody is picking

00:06:30.891 --> 00:06:31.891
up the old buildings.

00:06:32.431 --> 00:06:37.491
Amazon looked at Chile and Argentina more than eight years ago, and the rural site argument

00:06:37.491 --> 00:06:38.951
is at least a decade old.

00:06:39.711 --> 00:06:42.291
Hyperscalers are looking at remote South America again.

00:06:42.911 --> 00:06:44.131
The new part is shovels.

00:06:44.351 --> 00:06:49.271
Over the past few years, operators have broken ground on rural projects at a larger scale,

00:06:49.271 --> 00:06:52.631
pulled by power, land, and how communities answer.

00:06:53.191 --> 00:06:56.811
A workload that needs ultra low latency never makes that short list

00:06:56.811 --> 00:07:01.251
and neither does a build too small to pay for its own plant and its own fiber run.

